 "result": "I changed the reference query's primary key declaration from `[\"id\"]` to `[\"calendar_id\", \"id\"]`. The root cause of the errors was that ACL rule ids (`\"user:{email}\"`, matching the real API's rule-id format) are only unique *within* a calendar — the same `\"user:maya.chen@northwind.example\"` id legitimately exists on three different calendars (target, and both decoys) since Maya has a writer grant on each. With `key: [\"id\"]` the checker treated all three rows as the same witness (since they share an `id`), so it saw the target's `True` result bleed into the decoy checks and reported them as \"not killed\"/\"reused\". Declaring the true composite key (`calendar_id` + `id`) tells the checker these are distinct rows despite the shared `id` string, which matches how AclRule identity actually works in this domain (and keeps the id values realistic/write-compatible, since the write path relies on the exact `user:{email}` ruleId format).",
